# Block structure

Status: draft replacement for the block-structure clauses, implementing ruling [#3885](https://github.com/markup-carve/carve/issues/3885). The existing clauses stay normative until this section is approved and the spec model follows it. This section decides which container owns each line and where each block ends. The lexical productions give valid marker spellings; the inline and rendering clauses give what each resulting block contains.

## The rule

One rule decides ownership for every container:

> A container's content column is fixed by the line that opens it. A later line belongs to the deepest open container whose content column it reaches. Otherwise it continues an open paragraph lazily, or it closes containers.

No family, depth or marker adds a second way to belong. Nothing set after the opening line moves a content column.

## Input

Strip one leading byte order mark, replace nulls and ill-formed input with U+FFFD, and normalize line endings before splitting into lines. A blank line holds only spaces and tabs. Tabs expand to four-column stops for every column comparison; content keeps the authored characters. At the finite nesting cap (200 in the reference model) a further container opener is ordinary paragraph text.

## State

A parser holds the document frame, a path of open container frames, and at most one open leaf at the end of that path. Each frame records its marker column, its content column, its closer if it has one, and whether its deepest paragraph is open. Content columns:

|= Container       |= Content column                                                                            |
| Document         | 0                                                                                          |
| Quote            | after `>` and one optional space; a quote is reached by its marker, not by indentation     |
| List item        | after the marker and its separator; marker attributes do not count                         |
| Definition body  | after `:` and its separator                                                                |
| Footnote body    | two past the column of `[`                                                                 |
| Colon fence body | the content column of the owner the opener was read in, whatever space precedes the opener |

## Steps

For each line, in order:

1. *Payload.* If a code, raw or comment fence is open, test the line against its closer first. A code or raw fence with no matching closer ahead in its host also ends at the host's closer. Any other payload line opens nothing.
2. *Owner.* Walk the open frames from the outside in. A quote frame consumes its marker; any other frame consumes indentation up to its content column. Stop at the first frame the line does not reach; a lone `+` at the marker column of an open list item or quote reaches that frame (the `+` row). The owner is the deepest frame reached. A blank line reaches every indentation frame and no unmarked quote.
3. *Form.* Classify the rest of the line in the owner's coordinates: blank, comment, block attribute, reference or footnote definition, closer, opener, caption, `+`, or ordinary text. Leading space before an opener does not stop it from opening: an opener past the owner's content column opens in the owner, and its own lines are still measured from the owner's content column. A list marker at a list item's content column opens a child list; a sibling marker at the item's marker column continues the enclosing list.
4. *Lazy.* If the line missed a frame below its owner, it folds into the deepest open paragraph when that paragraph is open, the line is not blank, and its form does not interrupt a paragraph. The test is the same at every depth and in every family. A list marker interrupts only as the sibling of an open list. Headings, thematic breaks, quotes, terms, tables, valid colon openers, code openers with a reachable closer, comments, attributes and definitions interrupt. An invalid opener is text.
5. *Apply.* Otherwise close every missed frame and apply the form in the owner. A blank or invisible line ends the open paragraph. A container opener pushes a frame and classifies the rest of its marker line in that frame. A leaf opener ends the open leaf and starts a new one. Ordinary text starts or extends a paragraph. A closed block leaves no open paragraph. End of input closes every frame.

## Families

Every family below follows the five steps. The table gives only what a family adds: how it starts, what keeps it open, and what it renders.

|= Family                  |= Start                                                                                                                                                                                                 |= Kept open by                                                                          |= Lazy                                                           |= Blank line                                                                                                                                                                                                        |= End                                                                                                   |= Renders                                                                                   |
| List item                | A valid bullet, task, decimal, alphabetic, roman or bare-dot marker with content. A marker with no content is text. Same-column items group when marker kind, delimiter, dialect and task state agree. | A line reaching the content column.                                                    | Yes.                                                            | Keeps the item when a later line reaches it. A blank between items or before a second paragraph makes the list loose; a blank before a child block alone does not. Three blanks before a sibling start a new list. | Sibling marker, a missed line that does not fold, the host's end, end of input.                        | `<li>` in `<ul>` or `<ol>`; a task item carries a checkbox.                                |
| Term                     | `:: ` with content. A term is a leaf with inline content.                                                                                                                                              | A following line that would continue a paragraph in the same owner.                    | Yes, by the paragraph test.                                     | Ends the term.                                                                                                                                                                                                     | Anything that ends a paragraph, or a `: ` body marker in the same owner.                               | `<dt>` in `<dl>`.                                                                          |
| Definition body          | `: ` with content, directly after a term or body in the same owner.                                                                                                                                    | A line reaching the content column.                                                    | Yes.                                                            | Keeps the body when a later line reaches it. Paragraph separation makes the list loose.                                                                                                                            | Next term or body marker in the owner, a missed line that does not fold, the host's end, end of input. | `<dd>` in `<dl>`.                                                                          |
| Footnote body            | `[^label]: `.                                                                                                                                                                                          | A line reaching the content column.                                                    | Yes.                                                            | Keeps the body when a later line reaches it.                                                                                                                                                                       | A missed line that does not fold, the host's end, end of input.                                        | Collected; a referenced body renders as an endnote.                                        |
| Quote                    | `>` then a space or line end; each `>` opens one level.                                                                                                                                                | A line carrying the marker at that level.                                              | Yes.                                                            | An unmarked blank ends the quote; a marked blank keeps it and ends its paragraph.                                                                                                                                  | A missed marker that does not fold, the host's end, end of input.                                      | `<blockquote>`; a caption may wrap it as a figure.                                         |
| Colon fence              | A valid run of three or more colons; bare, typed, quoted, `figure`, `>`, `|` and backslash forms select the body mode.                                                                                 | Every line until the closer. `|` mode keeps lines as payload; other modes read blocks. | Block mode only.                                                | Content or block separation, by mode.                                                                                                                                                                              | An exact-length colon run read in the same owner, the host's end, end of input.                        | `<div>`, `<aside>`, figure group, quote, line block, hard-break block or placed directive. |
| Code, raw, comment fence | A valid code or raw opener; a comment opener with a matching closer.                                                                                                                                   | Every line until the closer; the payload is opaque.                                    | No.                                                             | Payload.                                                                                                                                                                                                           | A matching run, the host's end, end of input.                                                          | `<pre><code>`, the matching raw output, or nothing.                                        |
| Table                    | A valid pipe row, or a selected CSV or TSV fence. A continuation row needs a body row above it.                                                                                                        | The next valid row or row continuation.                                                | No.                                                             | Ends the table.                                                                                                                                                                                                    | The first line that is not a row, the host's end, end of input.                                        | `<table>`; an eligible caption may attach.                                                 |
| Caption                  | `^ ` with content after an image paragraph, quote, table, code block, display-math paragraph, figure-group closer or fenced-quote closer.                                                              | A line that would continue a paragraph.                                                | Yes; a second `^ ` ends it.                                     | One blank between host and marker is allowed, two detach. A blank after the caption ends it.                                                                                                                       | Anything that ends a paragraph, a second caption marker, the host's end.                               | `<figcaption>`, or `<caption>` on a table.                                                 |
| `+`                      | A lone `+` at the marker column of the deepest open list item or quote, the column on the line that opened it.                                                                                         | The next block, read in that container.                                                | None of its own; the attached block may leave a paragraph open. | A blank or a missing next block refuses it.                                                                                                                                                                        | The end of the one attached block.                                                                     | Nothing; the attached block stays in the container.                                        |

The marker column is the only place a `+` reaches, so it is the same column rule and not a second way to belong. A refused `+` is ordinary text in the owner step 2 selected, and folds only into a paragraph that is still open. The attached block is read in the container the `+` reached, never in another one.

## Leaves and invisible lines

Headings, thematic breaks, terms, paragraphs, reference and footnote definitions, comments and attribute lines are leaves or tokens, not containers. A comment or attribute line is invisible: it ends the open paragraph and leaves its owner open. A pending attribute belongs to its owner and expires when that owner closes. A collected definition has no visible placeholder. A structural line changes caption scope only when it opens or closes a container. Top-level heading sections are a rendering pass after ownership is settled.

## Retired

This section replaces PART 0 O1 to O4 and X1 to X3, PART 9 §10 I1 to I7, §17 L1 to L7 and §24 C1 to C5. Four behaviors those clauses carried are removed by #3885, and their rule ids retire with them:

|= Removed behavior                     |= Retired ids                                                                   |= What replaces it                                                                                                     |
| Authored block base                   | `CARVE-P0-004`                                                                 | Step 3: an opener past the content column opens in the owner; its lines are measured from the owner's content column. |
| Term ownership                        | `CARVE-P2-028`                                                                 | The term row: a term continues by the paragraph test and has no ownership of its own.                                 |
| `+` as a second way to belong         | `CARVE-P9-031`, `CARVE-P9-032`, `CARVE-P9-033`, `CARVE-P9-034`, `CARVE-P9-067` | The `+` row: a marker only at the marker column of the container step 2 selected.                                     |
| Depth- and family-specific lazy rules | `CARVE-P0-009`, `CARVE-P0-021`, `CARVE-P0-022`, `CARVE-P9-053`                 | Step 4: one lazy test at every depth.                                                                                 |

Every other id in the old clauses keeps its number and moves into the step or row named in the mapping of [#3877](https://github.com/markup-carve/carve/pull/3877).
